		<notes>
			<note>1743-06-28: Entered at Stationers' Hall in the names of Edmond Hoyle and Francis
				Cogan.</note>
			<note rend="paragraph"/>
			<note>I believe the preliminaries in an ideal copy are A</note>
			<note rend="super">2</note>
			<note> with the title page inserted after A1. That construction puts Hoyle's autograph as a frontispiece facing the title page, as is typical of the Cogan Hoyles.</note>
			<note rend="paragraph"/>
			<note>However, there are almost as many variations of the preliminaries as there are copies. The conjugacy of A1 and A2 is clear in some cpoies (CtY-BR and TxU), while the leaves have been separated in others (L, O [12 Theta 1318(3)]). Some copies have the title inserted before A1 (O [Jessel f.543(5)], O [Jessel f.579], O [Jessel f.580(2)], CtY-BR), others after (L, TxU). Some lack the title page althogether.</note>
			<note rend="paragraph"/>
			<note>Finally there is a variant in the text beginning on A2r. Some copies have "Where may be had, just published" rather than "Just published," although the setting of type is otherwise the same. </note>
		</notes>
